<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidyapati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1352–<a href="/years/1448">1448</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vidyapati was a poet and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, best known for his love songs in Maithili on Radha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. He served several kings of the Oiniwar dynasty, notably Shivasimha, and also wrote works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Avahatta. His lyrics influenced devotional poetry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and he is regarded as the foremost poet of the Maithili language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vidyapati.jpg" alt="Vidyapati" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bijay chaurasia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Maha_Kavi_Kokil_Vidyapati.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
